The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type | test, mask | test);
if (!alg && test)
    alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type, mask) ? ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD) : NULL;
```
Reason:
The first statement attempts to look up a crypto algorithm with a modified type and mask, which may include algorithms that are in a "larval" state (i.e., algorithms that are being tested but not yet fully initialized). The second statement incorrectly treats the absence of an algorithm as a failure, returning `ELIBBAD` even if the algorithm is merely in a larval state, leading to erroneous error handling when multiple processes access encrypted files.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (alg && !crypto_is_larval(alg)) {
    crypto_mod_put(alg);
    alg = ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD);
}
```
Reason:
These statements check if the found algorithm is not a larval algorithm before returning the `ELIBBAD` error. If the algorithm is larval, it does not treat it as a failure, thus preventing the erroneous return of the error code when multiple processes access the algorithm simultaneously. This ensures that only truly failed algorithms result in the `ELIBBAD` error, fixing the bug.

